-- allium: 3
--
-- JEBOL: DELECT, the parser every REBOL dialect is read by.
--
-- Scope: how a block of words and values becomes commands with their
-- arguments in a known order. One native, and the machinery behind it.
--
-- Why it comes before anything that uses it: DRAW, EFFECT, TEXT and REBCODE
-- are all read this way. {@code system/dialects} holds one object per dialect
-- and each object's fields are its commands, so writing a bespoke reader for
-- DRAW would be writing the first of four.
--
-- The idea worth stating plainly, because it is not how any other parser
-- works. A command declares the *types* of its arguments rather than their
-- order, and DELECT puts whatever it finds into whichever slot accepts it. So
-- {@code box 10x10 3.0 20x20} and {@code box 10x10 20x20 3.0} produce the same
-- answer, and a slot nobody filled comes back as none. That is what lets a
-- dialect read like a description instead of like a call.
--
-- Authorities: {@code src/core/u-dialect.c} is the whole implementation, 560
-- lines. {@code src/tests/units/delect-test.r3} is Rebol's own test and is the
-- third authority. Every expectation here was also run against a real 3.22.1
-- before it was written down.
--
-- Excludes:
--   - The dialects themselves. What DRAW's commands mean is
--     spec/draw.allium; this says only how the block is read.
--   - TRACE/DELECT's counting of missed arguments, which is debugging output
--     with no effect on what is parsed.

use "./values.allium" as values

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- How a value came to sit in a slot.
--
-- Two of the five are conversions rather than matches, and they are the only
-- place DELECT changes a value rather than moving it. Both are one-way and
-- neither is symmetric with the other: a decimal into a whole-number slot is
-- truncated, not rounded.
enum SlotFit {
    exactly              -- the value's type is the slot's type
    | a_whole_number_from_a_fraction  -- 3.7 into an integer slot becomes 3
    | a_fraction_from_a_whole_number  -- 4 into a decimal slot becomes 4.0
    | the_named_refinement            -- the word is the slot's own name
    | nothing_accepted_it             -- no slot would take it
}

-- What a command's declaration can say a slot is.
enum SlotKind {
    a_datatype       -- pair!, tuple!, integer!
    | a_typeset      -- any-string!, number!
    | a_refinement   -- a bare word that is its own name, such as `closed`
    | a_repeater     -- `*` before a type: as many of that type as are written
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One dialect: an object whose fields are its commands.
--
-- {@code system/dialects/draw} is one of these and so is anything a script
-- builds with {@code context [...]}. A field holding a block is a command and
-- the block is its slots; a field holding none is a keyword rather than a
-- command, which is how DRAW's option words -- {@code closed}, {@code
-- even-odd}, {@code bilinear} -- are known without being commands.
entity Dialect {
    commands: DialectCommand with dialect = this

    -- Every dialect has one, and values written before any command reach it.
    default_command: DialectCommand
}

entity DialectCommand {
    dialect: Dialect
    name: String
    slots: List<CommandSlot>

    -- A word whose field is none. It names something without taking anything.
    is_a_keyword: boolean
}

value CommandSlot {
    kind: SlotKind
    accepts: List<values.Datatype>
}

-- One call of DELECT: one command read, or the whole block when asked.
entity DelectRun {
    dialect: Dialect
    input: values.BlockStorage
    output: values.BlockStorage
    reads_the_whole_block: boolean      -- the /all refinement
    searches_contexts: boolean          -- the /in refinement
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Reading one command
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A command runs until the next command, or the end of the block.
--
-- Not until its slots are full, which is the difference that makes a dialect
-- readable. `box 10x10 20x20 circle 5x5 3` needs no punctuation between the
-- two commands because the reader stops at CIRCLE, and BOX's third slot is
-- simply never filled.
--
-- Only a command ends a command. A keyword does not, which is what lets
-- `spline 1x1 2x2 closed` finish with an option word.
rule ACommandRunsUntilTheNextOne {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: the_next_value_names_a_command(run)
    ensures: the_arguments_taken_are(everything_before_the_next_command(run))
}

-- Each argument goes into the first slot that will accept it, and the answer
-- is in slot order however the arguments were written.
--
-- The whole of what a dialect is for. Rebol's own test says it in one line:
-- with `cmd: [any-string! integer!]`, the input `cmd 3 a@b` answers
-- `[cmd a@b 3]`. Neither argument moved to where it was written; both went to
-- where they fit.
rule AnArgumentGoesToTheFirstSlotThatAcceptsIt {
    when: script_delects(run)
    ensures:
        for argument in the_arguments_taken(run):
            argument_sits_in(the_first_unfilled_slot_accepting(argument))
}

-- A slot nobody filled is none, and the command's answer is always as long as
-- its declaration.
--
-- Padded rather than truncated, because a reader of the answer counts along
-- the slots. A short answer would make the caller check the length before
-- every field, and a caller that forgot would read the wrong argument.
rule AnUnfilledSlotIsNone {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: some_slot_was_not_filled(run)
    ensures: every_unfilled_slot_holds(none)
}

-- The command's own word comes first in the answer.
rule TheAnswerBeginsWithTheCommand {
    when: script_delects(run)
    ensures: the_first_value_of(run.output) = the_command_that_was_read(run)
}

-- A command written as a lit-word answers as a lit-word.
--
-- `'box` rather than `box`, and it survives into the output. It is how a
-- caller marks a command it wants named rather than acted on, and losing the
-- mark would make the two indistinguishable to whatever reads the answer.
rule ALitWordCommandStaysALitWord {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: the_command_was_written_as_a_lit_word(run)
    ensures: the_first_value_of(run.output) = the_command_as_a_lit_word(run)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- What a value is, before it is placed
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A word that is not the dialect's own stands for whatever it holds.
--
-- Which is what lets a dialect be written with variables: `box origin size`
-- reads the two pairs those words hold. A word that *is* in the dialect is
-- never looked up, or every option word would have to be a defined variable.
rule AWordOutsideTheDialectIsLookedUp {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: some_argument_is_a_word_the_dialect_does_not_know(run)
    ensures: the_argument_placed_is(what_the_word_holds)
}

-- A paren is evaluated, so a dialect can carry a computed value.
rule AParenArgumentIsEvaluated {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: some_argument_is_a_paren(run)
    ensures: the_argument_placed_is(what_the_paren_answered)
}

-- A path is followed, and a lit-word becomes a plain word.
rule APathIsFollowedAndALitWordLosesItsMark {
    when: script_delects(run)
    ensures:
        for argument in the_arguments_taken(run) where is_a_path(argument):
            the_argument_placed_is(what_the_path_reached)
        for argument in the_arguments_taken(run) where is_a_lit_word(argument):
            the_argument_placed_is(the_same_word_without_its_mark)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Fitting a value to a slot
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A whole number offered to a fraction's slot becomes a fraction, and a
-- fraction offered to a whole number's slot is cut down to one.
--
-- Truncated and not rounded: `(REBI64)VAL_DECIMAL(value)`, so 3.7 becomes 3.
-- Worth pinning because rounding is the reasonable guess and it is wrong.
--
-- The two conversions are what make a dialect writable by hand. Nobody typing
-- `line-width 2` wants to be told it should have been `2.0`.
rule NumbersAreConvertedBetweenTheTwoNumericSlots {
    when: script_delects(run)
    ensures:
        for argument in the_arguments_taken(run):
            if a_fraction_meets_a_whole_number_slot(argument):
                the_argument_placed_is(the_fraction_with_its_part_cut_off)
            if a_whole_number_meets_a_fraction_slot(argument):
                the_argument_placed_is(the_whole_number_as_a_fraction)
}

-- A slot that names a word accepts that word and nothing else.
--
-- How DRAW's option words work: `spline: [integer! word! * pair!]` takes any
-- word, but a slot written as a bare name -- the refinement kind -- takes only
-- itself. Reaching one stops the search, so a word that is not it does not
-- fall through to a later slot.
rule ANamedSlotTakesOnlyItsOwnWord {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: some_slot_is_a_refinement(run)
    ensures:
        for argument in the_arguments_taken(run):
            if argument_is_that_slots_word(argument):
                argument_sits_in(that_slot)
}

-- A repeating slot takes as many as are written, and none when there are none.
--
-- `line: [* pair!]` and `polygon: [* pair!]` are how a dialect takes a list
-- without a block around it. A repeater that took nothing adds nothing, which
-- is the one place a slot does not pad with none: `many` on its own answers
-- `[many]` rather than `[many none]`.
rule ARepeatingSlotTakesAsManyAsAreWritten {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: some_slot_repeats(run)
    ensures:
        the_repeating_slot_holds(every_argument_of_that_type_in_a_row)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Values before any command
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Values written before a command reach the dialect's DEFAULT.
--
-- Which is what lets a dialect start with data. Its answer is named DEFAULT
-- like any other command, so a reader of the answer needs no special case.
rule ValuesBeforeACommandGoToTheDefault {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: the_next_value_does_not_name_a_command(run)
    ensures:
        the_command_that_was_read(run) = run.dialect.default_command
        one_value_is_taken(run)
}

-- A word the dialect does not know, that the default will not take either, is
-- refused.
--
-- Refused rather than skipped, and this is the decision in DELECT most worth
-- defending. A dialect is a description somebody typed, and a word nobody
-- serves is almost always a misspelling. Skipping it would draw a picture
-- missing one shape, with nothing anywhere saying which.
rule AWordNothingAcceptsIsRefused {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: the_next_value_does_not_name_a_command(run)
    requires: the_default_command_will_not_take_it(run)

    ensures:
        run.conclusion = raised
        run.error_id = invalid_arg
        the_error_names(run.input)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- What a call answers, and how far it got
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One call reads one command and answers the input standing after it.
--
-- Which is what makes a walk a loop: a caller passes the answer back in and
-- gets the next command. It answers none at the end of the block, so the loop
-- has something to stop on.
rule ACallAnswersTheInputAdvancedPastWhatItRead {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: not run.reads_the_whole_block
    ensures: DelectResult(value: run.input positioned after the command read)
}

rule AtTheEndOfTheBlockItAnswersNone {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: nothing_is_left_of(run.input)
    ensures: DelectResult(value: none)
}

-- The output block is rebuilt each call, not added to.
--
-- So a caller may keep one block and walk a whole dialect through it without
-- clearing it, and each command's answer stands alone.
rule TheOutputHoldsOnlyTheCommandJustRead {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: not run.reads_the_whole_block
    ensures: run.output = only_what_this_call_read(run)
}

-- Asked for the whole block, it reads every command into one answer.
--
-- The commands run together in the order they were written, each still
-- beginning with its own name, so the answer is a flat block a reader walks
-- once. That is what a renderer wants: one pass, no loop around the parser.
rule ReadingTheWholeBlockGathersEveryCommand {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: run.reads_the_whole_block
    ensures:
        for command in every_command_in(run.input):
            run.output holds command followed by its arguments
}

-- An output block that may not be changed is refused before anything is read.
rule AProtectedOutputIsRefused {
    when: script_delects(run)
    requires: the_output_is_protected(run)
    ensures: run.conclusion = raised
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- However the arguments were written, the answer is in slot order.
--
-- The one property everything downstream rests on. A renderer reading a DRAW
-- command counts along the slots, so an answer that was ever in written order
-- would put a radius where a corner belongs.
invariant TheAnswerIsAlwaysInSlotOrder {
    for run in DelectRun:
        for command in commands_read_by(run):
            the_values_after(command) line up with the_slots_of(command)
}

-- A call never reads past the command it answers for.
--
-- Or a walk would skip commands, and the caller would have no way to know: the
-- answer looks the same whether one command was read or three.
invariant ACallNeverReadsPastWhatItAnswersFor {
    for run in DelectRun:
        run.reads_the_whole_block or one_command_was_read(run)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surface
------------------------------------------------------------

surface TheDialectReader {
    facing script: DelectRun

    provides:
        Delect(script, dialect, input, output)
        DelectTheWholeBlock(script, dialect, input, output)

    exposes:
        SlotKind
        SlotFit

    @guarantee ADialectDeclaresTypesRatherThanAnOrder
        -- A command says what kinds of value it takes and DELECT puts each
        -- argument wherever it fits. So the same command can be written
        -- several ways and read one way, which is what makes a dialect read
        -- as a description rather than as a call with its arguments in a
        -- remembered sequence.
        --
        -- The cost is that two slots of the same type cannot be told apart by
        -- anything but their order, and a caller who writes them the wrong way
        -- round gets no complaint. DRAW lives with that: `box` takes two pairs
        -- and the first is the corner because it was written first.

    @guarantee OneReaderServesEveryDialect
        -- DRAW, EFFECT, TEXT and REBCODE are read by this and nothing else.
        -- A dialect is an object whose fields are its commands, so adding one
        -- is adding an object rather than adding a parser.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Two slots of the same datatype are told apart only by the order they were written in, and a caller who swaps them is not told. `box: [pair! pair! decimal!]` takes a corner and an end, and `box 20x20 10x10 0` is a backwards box that reads as valid. Nothing in the C checks it and nothing could without the dialect saying more than a type. Is this worth a warning somewhere, or is it the price of the whole idea?"

open question "What should a repeating slot do when a later slot could also have taken the value? The C stops repeating at the first value the repeater will not take and removes the unused output slot, then carries on with the next slot. That is faithful and it means `line: [* pair!]` followed by another slot is order-sensitive in a way the rest of the dialect is not. No dialect in the tree has a slot after a repeater, so this is currently theoretical."

open question "TRACE/DELECT counts arguments that found no slot and prints a running total. It changes nothing about what is parsed, so it is out of scope here. Is a count of near-misses worth having as anything other than debugging output -- could a dialect report `you wrote three things BOX could not use` rather than silently padding with none?"
